&lt;h2&gt;


  How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The stack is incredibly lightweight: &lt;strong&gt;Python, Streamlit, and Ollama.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I used &lt;strong&gt;Gemma 3 (4B)&lt;/strong&gt;, an open-weight vision model, served locally via &lt;strong&gt;Ollama&lt;/strong&gt;. The Streamlit frontend communicates with the local Ollama server via &lt;code&gt;localhost:11434&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the user clicks "Generate," the app reads the image bytes, packages them with a carefully engineered prompt (which includes the selected "voice"), and sends them to Ollama. The app uses &lt;code&gt;ollama.chat(..., stream=True)&lt;/code&gt; so the text renders progressively on the screen rather than making the user stare at a blank loading screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because I built this for real people, I also focused heavily on &lt;strong&gt;error handling&lt;/strong&gt;. Here is what happens when the user doesn't have Ollama running, and what the one-time ~3.3GB model download looks like:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ff3xw4wp8jj403eigyitb.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ff3xw4wp8jj403eigyitb.png" alt="Ollama not running in the background" width="800" height="527"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj00pze8sf9dl9rpf7hdr.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj00pze8sf9dl9rpf7hdr.png" alt="One Time model download" width="800" height="529"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For this specific use case, &lt;strong&gt;closed APIs are a dealbreaker.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Privacy &amp;amp; Unreleased Inventory:&lt;/strong&gt; Sellers often list handmade items or vintage finds they haven't publicly announced yet. Uploading those photos to a third-party API (like OpenAI or Anthropic) means that data is on a server they don't control. With local open weights, the photo never leaves the laptop.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Zero Marginal Cost:&lt;/strong&gt; A seller listing 200 items a month would be billed for 200 vision API calls. With an open model running locally, the cost of generating 1 or 1,000 listings is exactly $0. The only cost is the electricity to run the laptop.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;True Offline Capability:&lt;/strong&gt; Sellers source inventory at flea markets, warehouses, and craft fairs—places with terrible WiFi. A closed API fails completely without internet. My tool works completely offline after the initial one-time model download.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;No Lock-in:&lt;/strong&gt; If Gemma 3 isn't good enough for a specific niche, I can swap the &lt;code&gt;model="gemma3:4b"&lt;/code&gt; string to &lt;code&gt;llama3.2-vision&lt;/code&gt; in a single line of code. I am not locked into one provider's pricing changes or deprecations.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Bu yazının Türkçesi: &lt;a href="https://onurbaydas.com/posts/reservation-session-control/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Paylaşımlı Linux İş İstasyonlarında Süreli Giriş&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You have a room of shared Linux workstations. They are joined to Active Directory, users sign in with their domain accounts, and the machines are too valuable to be first come, first served. People book a specific machine for a specific window: Tuesday 09:00 to Thursday 18:00 on &lt;code&gt;ws07&lt;/code&gt;, for example. The system has to enforce three rules:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nobody signs in without a valid reservation for this machine, right now.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;When the reservation ends, the session ends.&lt;/strong&gt; Not at the next logout, and not when someone notices.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;An administrator can change things centrally.&lt;/strong&gt; Cancel, extend, put a machine into maintenance, ban a user, and see the effect within a minute or two.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first rule is about &lt;em&gt;admission&lt;/em&gt;. The second is about &lt;em&gt;eviction&lt;/em&gt;. The third is about &lt;em&gt;who owns the truth&lt;/em&gt;. Most tools you can find solve the first rule, and solve it well. Hardly any of them solve the second. For the third, you usually end up editing a config file on every host.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post covers the architecture I built for it (a PAM login gate, SSSD, a session watchdog, a heartbeat, and a central reservation API), the trade-offs I accepted, and the edge cases a lab rebuild exposed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Off-the-Shelf Options and Why They Fall Short
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before writing any code, I compared what already exists on the seven properties this problem needs: dynamic reservations, machine-specific assignment, expiry in the middle of a session, advance warning, central revoke and extend, behavior during a network outage, and an audit trail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
