&lt;h1&gt;


  Vaultwarden at internet scale: 87,252 title matches on the self-hosted password vault
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Vaultwarden is the lightweight, community-maintained implementation of the Bitwarden server API, and it is a popular choice for teams that want password management on their own hardware. A ZoomEye search for the product's page title returns 87,252 matches, which is a large number for a service whose entire purpose is to hold credentials.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How the figure was produced
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The measurement is a single ZoomEye query with the condition &lt;code&gt;title="Vaultwarden"&lt;/code&gt;, run with &lt;code&gt;sub_type=all&lt;/code&gt; and a page size of one. Page size limits how many records are returned; it does not limit the matched total, which is what the platform reports for the condition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three qualifications belong with the number. It is a title match, so it reflects hosts where the string appears in the page title at the moment of indexing, and it does not verify that the instance is reachable now. It does not distinguish a serious deployment from a test instance someone left running. And it counts hosts, not accounts, so the population of credentials behind those hosts is unknown from the outside.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even with those caveats, 87,252 is the kind of figure that changes the question from whether exposure exists to how much of it is intentional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What a reachable Vaultwarden instance exposes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first thing a scanner sees is usually the web vault interface, which is designed to be reached by users and therefore sits on a normal HTTPS port. Behind it are the API endpoints the clients use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The security model depends on the master password and on the server never seeing the derived key in a usable form. That design is sound, and it means an exposed instance does not immediately leak vault contents. What it does expose is the identity layer. Registration is frequently left open so that new employees can enroll themselves, and on an open instance an attacker can create an account, observe the invitation and organisation behaviour, and use the login endpoint as a credential-stuffing oracle against real user addresses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The administrative surface is the second concern. Vaultwarden ships an &lt;code&gt;/admin&lt;/code&gt; panel that is protected by a token. Instances where the token was left at a default or committed to a configuration repository have been the subject of past disclosures in this ecosystem, and the panel exposes user lists, organisation settings and SMTP configuration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Reading the number usefully
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The value of 87,252 is comparison, not alarm. Run the same condition against the organisation's own discovery output and the difference between the two numbers is the set of hosts the organisation does not know about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For the ones that are known, three checks take an afternoon. Confirm that registration is closed, or that it requires an invitation. Confirm the admin token was generated for this deployment rather than copied from a guide. And confirm the instance is on the version the project currently recommends, since the project publishes security-relevant fixes with its releases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What ZoomEye adds here
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A title query answers one question: how many hosts announce this product. The follow-on queries are where the measurement becomes a management tool. Filtering by country narrows the population to regions the organisation operates in. Filtering by the certificate in use, where the field is available, separates hosts with a real certificate from those with a default one, which is a rough proxy for whether anyone is maintaining the instance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Used that way, the figure moves from a headline to a starting list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The count reflects indexed fingerprints and not verified live services, and it does not measure whether any instance has been compromised. Nothing in this article is a claim about a specific organisation's exposure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ZoomEye search syntax and field reference: &lt;a href="https://www.zoomeye.ai/doc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.zoomeye.ai/doc&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mistral's new forecasting model has 209M parameters, and Mistral says it forecasts a day of 15-minute data in 140 ms on an 8-thread server CPU. According to the model card, Timestral-209M also tops GIFT-Eval, TIME and fev-bench, ahead of TimesFM-3 and Chronos-2. It's zero-shot, so you don't train anything: hand it a series and you get 21 quantiles back. The question for anyone running Prophet, ARIMA or Chronos today is whether any of that holds up off the vendor's slides, and what the license lets you do with it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've spent a few evenings with the model card, the &lt;a href="https://mistral.ai/news/timestral/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;announcement&lt;/a&gt;, the &lt;a href="https://github.com/mistralai/timestral" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;repo&lt;/a&gt; and the license. Below is what the 140 ms CPU number actually covers and a Python snippet that forecasts a CSV with it. The launch post leaves out two things: Moirai is missing from the comparison, and the license has a $20M revenue clause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Timestral specs: context length, horizon, size
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Timestral was released on October 15, 2026, in four sizes (5M, 28M, 123M, 209M) that share one architecture and training recipe. These numbers are for the 209M checkpoint, taken from the &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Timestral-209M" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Hugging Face model card&lt;/a&gt; and the README:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Spec&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Timestral-209M&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Architecture&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Patch-based transformer, 24 layers, embedding dim 768&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Patch size&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;32 time steps&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Max context&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;32,768 steps (1,024 patches)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Horizon per forward pass&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,024 steps; longer horizons run block by block, feeding each block's median back in as context&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Output&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;21 quantiles, 0.01 to 0.99&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Inputs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Univariate or multivariate targets, past-only and known-future covariates (numeric or categorical), NaN for missing values&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Weights&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;837 MB fp32&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Requirements&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Python ≥ 3.10, torch ≥ 2.4&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;License&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Code Apache 2.0, weights "Modified MIT"&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mistral says most of the training data is synthetic. They generated univariate processes with trend, seasonality and noise, then combined them into multivariate series with nonlinear dependencies. Pre-training ran on synthetic data only at context 8192, and post-training used a real/synthetic mix with a 4x longer context. The technical report is still "coming soon."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How Timestral compares with Chronos-2 and TimesFM-3
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These numbers are from the model card. Lower is better for MASE and CRPS; higher is better for the fev-bench skill scores.&lt;/p&gt;

