&lt;p&gt;How I read that table:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On GIFT-Eval MASE, Timestral leads t0-gamma by 0.003 and TimesFM-3 by 0.009. That's within the range where your own data will decide the winner. The margin over Chronos-2 and Toto is bigger and harder to wave away.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Toto-2.0 has 2.5B parameters and finishes behind the 209M model on every row. If you care about inference cost, that's the most useful line in the table.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Moirai isn't in it. The model card doesn't list it and the announcement doesn't mention it. If Moirai is what you run today, you'll have to check the &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/Salesforce/GIFT-Eval" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GIFT-Eval&lt;/a&gt; leaderboard yourself or run a backtest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;These are all vendor-reported scores. The results were submitted to the GIFT-Eval and TIME leaderboards, and the second issue on the repo, opened the day after launch, is titled &lt;a href="https://github.com/mistralai/timestral/issues" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;"Verify evals on Papers with Code"&lt;/a&gt;. Until the tech report and independent runs exist, I'd treat "first on all three" as a strong claim that hasn't been checked yet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The family table is more useful for picking a size. The 123M model scores 0.659 GIFT-Eval MASE against 0.658 for the 209M. Mistral says it's "within 0.2%" of the 209M at about three quarters of the latency. The 28M is about five times faster than the 209M and, per Mistral, already beats Chronos-2 on GIFT-Eval and TIME. If you're forecasting thousands of series every hour, start with the 28M.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Does Timestral really run on a laptop CPU?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The one hard latency number comes from the &lt;a href="https://mistral.ai/news/timestral/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;announcement&lt;/a&gt;: on "a standard server CPU with 8 threads," forecasting one day ahead from three weeks of 15-minute history took 10 ms for the 5M model and 140 ms for the 209M. The README says "CPU-friendly: no GPU needed for inference" but gives no memory figures or laptop timings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So "runs on CPU" is well supported. "Runs on a laptop" means 837 MB of fp32 weights plus activations, which any recent dev machine can hold. Long contexts near 32K steps will cost more than that 140 ms figure, and nobody has published timings for them yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Forecast a CSV with Timestral in Python
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Install from GitHub (there's no PyPI package mentioned):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;pip &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install &lt;/span&gt;git+https://github.com/mistralai/timestral.git
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The API takes NumPy arrays or torch tensors shaped &lt;code&gt;(T,)&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;(B, T)&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;(B, V, T)&lt;/code&gt;. It doesn't accept DataFrames yet; &lt;a href="https://github.com/mistralai/timestral/issues" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;issue #1&lt;/a&gt; asks for pandas/Polars support. That means you load the CSV with pandas yourself:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;numpy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pandas&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pd&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timestral&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;TimestralForecaster&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# daily_sales.csv: columns "date", "sales"
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;read_csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;daily_sales.csv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parse_dates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set_index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;series&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;df&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;sales&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;asfreq&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# regular grid; gaps become NaN, which Timestral treats as missing
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;TimestralForecaster&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;from_pretrained&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;mistralai/Timestral-209M&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;device&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;cuda&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;torch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;cuda&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;is_available&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;cpu&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forecast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;series&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;to_numpy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dtype&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;float64&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;copy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;prediction_length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;28&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;median&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;asarray&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;median&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;squeeze&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;lower&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;asarray&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;quantile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;squeeze&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;upper&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;np&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;asarray&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;forecast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;quantile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;squeeze&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;future_idx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;date_range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;series&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Timedelta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;periods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;28&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;freq&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;out&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DataFrame&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;p10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lower&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;p50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;median&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;p90&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;upper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;index&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;future_idx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;out&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;head&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The script picks the CPU when there's no CUDA device, and &lt;code&gt;asfreq("D")&lt;/code&gt; turns any skipped dates into NaN so the model sees them as missing rather than as a shorter series. The &lt;code&gt;copy=True&lt;/code&gt; is there because recent pandas can hand back a read-only array, and PyTorch warns about that when it wraps it in a tensor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything Timestral-specific in that snippet (&lt;code&gt;from_pretrained&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;forecast&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.median&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;.quantile()&lt;/code&gt;, NaN for missing values) comes from the model card and README. The &lt;code&gt;squeeze()&lt;/code&gt; calls are there because the README documents &lt;code&gt;median&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;quantile(q)&lt;/code&gt; as shape &lt;code&gt;(B, V, horizon)&lt;/code&gt;, so even a 1-D input comes back with batch and variate axes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A few more things from the README you'll want in production:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run it offline: &lt;code&gt;from_pretrained&lt;/code&gt; accepts &lt;code&gt;revision=&lt;/code&gt; to pin a commit and &lt;code&gt;local_files_only=True&lt;/code&gt; (or &lt;code&gt;HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1&lt;/code&gt;) to read from cache. Do this in CI and in containers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Covariates: &lt;code&gt;past_covariates&lt;/code&gt; has shape &lt;code&gt;(B, C_past, T)&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;known_covariates&lt;/code&gt; has shape &lt;code&gt;(B, C_known, T + horizon)&lt;/code&gt; and covers things like promotions, holidays or production schedules. Categorical codes go into &lt;code&gt;categorical_known_covariates&lt;/code&gt; (the README suggests &lt;code&gt;pandas.factorize&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Context is capped at 32,768 steps and the most recent ones are kept, so you can pass the whole history without trimming it yourself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The Timestral license has a $20M revenue cap
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The weights use a &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Timestral-209M/blob/main/LICENSE" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;"Modified MIT" license&lt;/a&gt;. It keeps the MIT permissions and adds one condition: you lose the rights if your company's (or your employer's) global consolidated monthly revenue was over $20 million in the preceding month. Above that line you need a commercial license from Mistral or have to use its hosted services, and the restriction carries over to derivatives and fine-tunes, including ones made by third parties.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a startup or a side project this doesn't matter. If you're a contractor building forecasting for a large retailer, it does. Read the license before you ship this inside a client's stack. The repo code is Apache 2.0, but the code isn't the part that produces the forecasts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Should you replace Prophet with Timestral?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wouldn't replace anything yet. I'd add Timestral as a challenger. Take a year of your own metrics and backtest Timestral-28M and 209M against whatever you run now, on rolling windows, using MASE and pinball loss. If it wins, you've dropped per-series tuning, and that's where most of the cost of a Prophet or ARIMA setup goes. If it loses on your two most important series, you've learned that in an afternoon instead of in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two signs I'd watch before trusting it with alerts: the tech report and independent leaderboard runs. Neither exists yet, and the model is days old.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Would you trust a zero-shot foundation model over a tuned Prophet or ARIMA for your production metrics? If you've already backtested Chronos or TimesFM against your own data, tell me which one won and by how much. I'm especially interested in the cases where the boring model won.&lt;/p&gt;

